# Comprehensive Analysis of Cybersecurity Trends and Risks (2026)

## Executive Summary

Recent cybersecurity trends demonstrate concerning advances in the speed and sophistication of cyber threats. In 2025, attacks conducted by artificial intelligence (AI)-enabled adversaries increased by **89%**, with threat actors exploiting legitimate AI tools to generate malicious commands.

The average compromise time in cybercrime incidents fell to just **29 minutes**, representing a 65% increase in speed compared with 2024. In exceptional cases, attackers reportedly achieved full system control within only **27 seconds**.

Meanwhile, ransomware and Distributed Denial-of-Service (DDoS) attacks remain dominant threats across the European Union (EU).

From a regulatory perspective, the EU Artificial Intelligence Act (AI Act) was scheduled to become broadly applicable on 2 August 2026, while Portugal's transposition of the Network and Information Security Directive (NIS2) was expected to be completed during 2026, introducing more demanding obligations for businesses and public-sector organisations.

Immediate priorities include strengthening cyber threat intelligence capabilities, incorporating AI-enabled detection technologies, reinforcing perimeter security and risk management, and preparing for compliance with emerging regulatory requirements.

**Sources:** [CrowdStrike Global Threat Report 2026](https://www.crowdstrike.com/pt-br/global-threat-report/) | [ENISA Threat Landscape](https://www.enisa.europa.eu/news/exploring-the-evolution-of-the-cyber-threat-landscape-how-dependencies-weaken-our-digital-resilience)

