## 2. Global Cyber Threat Landscape (2025-2026)

The CrowdStrike Global Threat Report 2026 indicates that 2025 was characterised by increasingly rapid and automated cyberattacks.

### 2.1 Acceleration of Cyberattacks

The average time to compromise in cybercrime incidents reportedly fell to **29 minutes**, representing a 65% increase in speed compared with 2024.

In exceptional cases, attackers were reported to have achieved full system control in just **27 seconds**.

This acceleration substantially reduces the time available for defensive intervention, increasing the importance of real-time detection technologies, automated responses and continuous security monitoring.

### 2.2 Increased Use of Artificial Intelligence by Threat Actors

The report identified an **89% increase in attacks conducted by AI-enabled adversaries**.

CrowdStrike describes artificial intelligence as both a force multiplier for cyberattacks and an additional attack surface.

Legitimate AI tools were reportedly exploited to generate malicious code and extract sensitive information from more than 90 organisations.

Another finding was that **ChatGPT was mentioned 550% more frequently in criminal forums than any other AI model**, indicating substantial cybercriminal interest in generative artificial intelligence.

Threat actors are increasingly integrating AI into phishing campaigns, social engineering and intrusion strategies, expanding the efficiency and potential scale of malicious operations.

### 2.3 Fileless Attacks and Vulnerability Exploitation

In 2025:

- **82% of detected intrusions were malware-free**, reflecting the increasing exploitation of legitimate system tools and malicious scripts.
- Exploitation of zero-day vulnerabilities before public disclosure reportedly increased by **42%**.

These developments highlight the limitations of traditional antivirus controls when attackers exploit legitimate tools, credentials and system functionality.

### 2.4 Ransomware and Conventional Cyber Threats

Ransomware continues to represent one of the most consequential categories of cyber incidents in the short term across the EU.

Data exfiltration and system-encryption attacks remain major sources of operational and financial losses.

CrowdStrike also reported **US$1.46 billion in cryptocurrency theft during 2025**.

Politically motivated hacktivism and DDoS campaigns have increased, particularly against public-sector organisations and essential infrastructure.

### 2.5 Cross-Domain Movement and Edge Infrastructure

Threat actors increasingly exploit security visibility gaps and move across different technology environments, including identity infrastructure, cloud systems and virtualised environments.

CrowdStrike describes attackers as moving fluidly across these domains to bypass traditional endpoint-focused detection.

Additional findings for 2025 include:

- **40% of vulnerabilities exploited by China-linked threat actors targeted edge devices**, including Internet of Things (IoT) infrastructure.
- Cloud-aware intrusions attributed to state-linked adversaries reportedly increased by **266%**.

These findings point to increasing attacker attention towards cloud infrastructure, edge devices and hybrid technology environments.

**Source:** [CrowdStrike Global Threat Report 2026](https://www.crowdstrike.com/pt-br/global-threat-report/)

