## 4. Main Threat Actors and Affected Sectors

According to the European Union Agency for Cybersecurity (ENISA), **73% of targeted organisations in the analysed incident dataset were classified as essential or important entities under NIS2**.

Public administration remained the most frequently targeted sector, accounting for **32% of reported cases**.

Ideologically motivated DDoS campaigns represented a substantial proportion of attacks affecting public administrations, reaching 82% in the dataset described by the original report.

Other frequently targeted sectors included:

| Sector | Share of reported cases |
|---|---:|
| Public administration | 32% |
| Business services | 8% |
| Transport | 8% |
| Manufacturing | 7% |
| Finance and banking | 6% |

### Third-Party and Supply-Chain Exposure

Increasing dependence on external technology providers expands the attack surface of organisations across sectors.

ENISA highlights the operational consequences of supply-chain compromises and attacks involving third-party service providers.

Such incidents can affect multiple organisations simultaneously, resulting in large-scale disruption.

### Portuguese Context

In Portugal, the National Cybersecurity Centre (Centro Nacional de Cibersegurança - CNCS) coordinates cybersecurity initiatives, supports incident response and promotes awareness through events such as the C-Days conference.

The original report characterised cybersecurity maturity across some essential sectors, including energy, healthcare and telecommunications, as ranging from medium to low.

This characterisation was not accompanied by a specific supporting dataset in the original report.

**Source:** [ENISA - Exploring the Evolution of the Cyber Threat Landscape](https://www.enisa.europa.eu/news/exploring-the-evolution-of-the-cyber-threat-landscape-how-dependencies-weaken-our-digital-resilience)

